PRoot's faked hard links: count a link down only when the call removing it succeeded.

With --link2symlink, PRoot fakes hard links with symlinks and keeps the link count in the
name of a hidden file, deleting that file when the count reaches zero. It counted a link down
on entry to the rename(2) or unlink(2) that removes or replaces it, before knowing whether
the call would succeed, so a call that failed still counted it down:

 - rename onto an existing faked hard link that fails (RENAME_NOREPLACE: "File exists")
   counted it down; with two links, removing the other one then deleted the data;
 - on x86-64 Android, whose seccomp filter blocks rename(2) and unlink(2) (bionic uses the
   *at calls), PRoot runs the call again as renameat(2) or unlinkat(2), so its entry was seen
   twice and the link counted down twice. dpkg stopped on it ("unable to securely remove
   '/usr/bin/perl.dpkg-tmp': Operation not permitted").

Remember the link on entry and count it down at exit when the kernel says the call succeeded
(its raw result, before PRoot's own exit processing, which can turn the result of a call the
filter skipped into 0).

